Representations of the same relationship give the same $y$-value for every shared $x$-value.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.7]
\draw[->,thick] (-0.4,0)--(5.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,thick] (0,-0.4)--(0,5.2) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue!70!black] (-0.3,0.2)--(4.4,4.9);
\fill (0,0.5) circle (2.2pt);
\fill (1,1.5) circle (2.2pt);
\fill (2,2.5) circle (2.2pt);
\fill (3,3.5) circle (2.2pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

One matching point is not enough: two different lines can still cross at a single shared point.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.7]
\draw[->,thick] (-0.4,0)--(5.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,thick] (0,-0.4)--(0,5.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue!70!black] (0,1)--(4,5);
\draw[thick,red!70!black] (0,4.5)--(4,1.5);
\fill (2,3) circle (2.2pt);
\node[font=\footnotesize] at (2,3.6) {$P$};
\node[font=\footnotesize,blue!70!black] at (4.6,4.9) {$\ell_1$};
\node[font=\footnotesize,red!70!black] at (4.6,1.3) {$\ell_2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

